  "id": "Compound::DB00511 - Gene::486",
  "verbalization": "Acetyldigitoxin, a cardiotonic steroid, is shown to bind to Atp1b3 and Atp1a3, both of which are involved in sodium-potassium ATPase activity. This enzyme is critical for maintaining cellular potassium homeostasis, and its inhibition is linked to the regulation of cellular function in various tissues. The evidence suggests that Acetyldigitoxin may modulate Fxyd2 through its interaction with these ATPases, as Fxyd2 is a participant in the potassium-transporting ATPase activity. The consistent binding of Acetyldigitoxin to Atp1b3 and Atp1a3, along with the shared participation in potassium transport, indicates a potential mechanism by which the drug influences Fxyd2. The observed final scores suggest a strong, biologically plausible relationship, supported by the shared functional role of these ATPases in potassium transport. The mechanism is further supported by the presence of multiple paths linking Acetyldigitoxin to Fxyd2 through the same pathway, reinforcing the likelihood of a direct or indirect interaction.",
